  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-08-24T11:33:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "a41f3e7c-9d67-4702-b125-a7f0fe54b951",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 241133\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n433 AM PDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Hotter than normal temperatures will continue through the week.\n\n* Best chance of storms along and south of I-15 today, with \n  diminishing odds afterwards.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the weekend.\n\nA slight cooldown is anticipated today and Tuesday thanks to a \nsubtle uptick in moisture and fall in 500mb heights. Highs during \nthis time are forecast to be 1-5 degrees cooler than yesterday \n(largest cooldown across the southeastern half of the CWA), but \nstill above normal for late August. From Wednesday through \nFriday, ensemble guidance suggests an uptick in 500mb heights and \nsouthwesterly flow, the latter yielding a gradual reduction of \nmoisture. The net effect of these changes is warming at the \nsurface, with highs returning to values 3-8 degrees above normal. \nCombined with oppressive overnight lows, major to extreme heatrisk\nresumes across most of the Mojave Desert. Over the weekend (and \ninto early next week), ensembles depict a broader trough \napproaching the western US, leading to cooler temperatures and \nincreased breezes.\n\nOvernight radar and satellite imagery shows a complex of storms in \nsouthern AZ pushing our way. Convection should wane before it \nreaches our area, but the southerly outflow will advect in \nadditional moisture through the early morning hours. Latest HREF \nshows the 1\" PWAT contour extending up to the I-15 corridor this \nafternoon, which should allow isolated to scattered storms in this\narea. Main concerns will be gusty outflow winds and dry \nlightning, but cannot rule out isolated instances of flash \nflooding. Moisture should then gradually decrease throughout the \nweek, leading to more isolated and weaker activity. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...At\nissuance, an outflow boundary from storms in Arizona is moving \nnorthwestward, expected to result in a southeasterly wind shift at\nthe terminal early in the period. Southeasterly winds around \n8-10KT are expected to persist through late morning, then veer to \nthe southwest. Outside of convective influences, winds will be \nsimilar to yesterday, with speeds 12-15KT and gusts to 20-25KT \nthrough sunset. However, there is a 10-20 percent chance of \nthunderstorms to develop over the higher terrain to the east and \nsouth of the Valley with potential outflow impacts at the \nterminal, thus there is moderate confidence in the wind forecast. \nOutflow will remain a concern through the evening, with winds \nexpected to become light and variable overnight. VFR conditions \nwill prevail with FEW-SCT clouds with bases at or above 12kft. \nTemperatures will exceed 100 degrees today from 18Z-02Z, with a \nhigh of 107 degrees.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Gusty southerly to\nsoutheasterly winds have developed through the Lower Colorado \nRiver Valley and across NW Arizona early in the period due to \noutflow, with southeasterly winds expected to develop in the Las \nVegas Valley early in the period. This afternoon, winds will shift\nto the south and southwest, with speeds 10-15KT and gusts 20-25KT\ncommon, outside of convective influences. Isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms are expected this afternoon, mainly along and \nsouth/east of I-15, with higher confidence over northwestern \nArizona and potential impacts at KIFP/KEED. Outflow will remain a \nconcern through the evening elsewhere, and trends will be \nmonitored. At KDAG/across the western Mojave Desert, winds will \ngenerally remain around 10-15KT, veering throughout the day. In \nthe northern Owens Valley/KBIH, gusty up-valley winds veer to the \nsouthwest late afternoon before shifting north in the evening. VFR\nconditions prevail, with FEW-SCT clouds at or above 10kft.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
